# Global Rules for Figma to Responsive Web Translation

Whenever you are asked to convert Figma CSS into web code, you MUST apply logical, responsive web development practices and NEVER blindly copy fixed dimensions or absolute positions.

1. TYPOGRAPHY SCALING (rem) — GLOBAL HIERARCHY:
Translate Figma pixel sizes into responsive `rem` units. Do NOT copy oversized Figma type. All existing and future pages MUST use this harmonious scale relative to a **16px body** (`1rem` at the default root). Never use headings that dwarf the body text.

* **Body text (טקסט רץ / `p`, instructional copy, lists):** Strictly **16px** (`1rem`). Line-height **1.6–1.75**. Buttons and nav labels also use `1rem` unless they sit inside a heading.
* **Heading level 3 (`h3`):** **20px–22px** (`1.25rem`–`1.375rem`), font-weight **600 or 700**.
* **Heading level 2 (`h2`):** **28px–32px** (`1.75rem`–`2rem`), font-weight **700**.
* **Heading level 1 (`h1` / unit title):** **40px–44px** (`2.5rem`–`2.75rem`), font-weight **700**. On small viewports, scale down within this system (e.g. `h1` toward `2rem`, `h2` toward `1.5rem`) — never jump to a larger, disconnected size.

Keep these values in `:root` typography tokens and reuse them. Ignore Figma type that is 50px+ or otherwise out of proportion; map it down onto this scale.

2. BUTTONS, TAGS & CONTAINERS:
* NEVER use fixed `width` or `height` for buttons, badges, or text containers.
* Use `padding` (e.g., `padding: 0.5rem 1.5rem;`) and `display: inline-flex` to allow elements to size naturally around their content.
* If multiple badges/tags appear together, apply a consistent `min-width` so they look uniform, rather than fixing their exact width.
* Preserve `border-radius` and `background-color` values from Figma.

3. LAYOUT & POSITIONING:
* IGNORE all `position: absolute`, `left`, `top`, `bottom`, `right` properties from Figma.
* IGNORE desktop-sized fixed widths (e.g., `width: 1440px`).
* Structure the layout using modern CSS Flexbox and CSS Grid.
* Ensure all sections are responsive with media queries for mobile viewing.

4. RTL DIRECTION:
* This project is in Hebrew. Always structure the HTML with `<html lang="he" dir="rtl">`.
* Ensure Flexbox behaviors, margins, and text alignments naturally fit right-to-left reading.

5. E-LEARNING & PROGRESS TRACKING:
* Whenever the user asks to save progress, track completion, or remember where a user stopped, exclusively use vanilla JavaScript `localStorage`.
* Do NOT suggest backend databases, APIs, or user authentication systems, as this is a static site without logged-in users.

6. MEDIA & EXTERNAL EMBEDS:
* For Vimeo videos, always generate responsive iframe wrappers using CSS `aspect-ratio: 16/9` to ensure they scale correctly on mobile.
* For interactive elements like Storyline or H5P, generate standard `iframe` tags within styled container `div`s, setting `width: 100%` and removing default borders.

7. UI/UX MICRO-INTERACTIONS:
* Automatically add smooth CSS transitions (`transition: all 0.3s ease;`) to all interactive elements (buttons, cards, links) for hover states.
* Ensure all clickable elements receive `cursor: pointer`.
* Provide accessible `:focus-visible` outlines for keyboard navigation.

8. CODE GENERATION RULES:
* Stick to vanilla HTML, CSS, and JS. Do not import React, Vue, Tailwind, or Bootstrap.
* Keep the CSS architecture modular: define colors and typography variables in `:root` at the top, followed by layout, and then component-specific classes.

9. IMAGES, ICONS & TRANSPARENCY:
* Transparency: NEVER apply `background` or `background-color` to `<img>` tags or their immediate wrapper `div`s (like `.icon-container`). Figma frames often export with default solid backgrounds (e.g., grey/white); ignore these and ensure image backgrounds remain transparent.
* Proportions & Aspect Ratio: Never distort images. Always use `height: auto;` and `max-width: 100%;` to maintain original proportions.
* Image resizing: Whenever you change an image’s displayed size (width, height, max-width, max-height, or a bounding box), you MUST keep its original aspect ratio. Scale uniformly. NEVER squash or stretch. Do not use `object-fit: fill`, mismatched width/height, or `background-size` values that change the ratio. Prefer `object-fit: contain` if a box is required. Apply this to `<img>`, CSS backgrounds, icons, and decorative graphics.
* Flexbox Squashing: If an image or icon is placed inside a Flexbox container, always apply `flex-shrink: 0;` to the image so it doesn't get stretched or squashed by other elements. Use `object-fit: contain;` if a fixed bounding box is absolutely necessary.

* SCROLLING BACKGROUNDS: When applying a background image to the page, it MUST scroll naturally with the content. 
1. NEVER use `position: fixed` for background wrappers. 
2. ALWAYS use `background-attachment: scroll;` (or let it default).
3. Ensure the background spans the full document height so the user can scroll to the bottom of the image (e.g., applying it to the `body` or a full-height wrapper with `background-size: 100% auto;` or `cover;`).

10. OPENING PROSE LINE LENGTH (~20 WORDS):
* Outside interactive components (sticky notes, carousel cards, partner cards, quotes inside an activity, buttons), opening paragraphs, lead text, and instructional body copy MUST wrap so that no line holds more than about 20 Hebrew words.
* Constrain those paragraphs with `max-width: 47em` (relative to that paragraph’s `font-size`), keep `text-align: right` in RTL, and do not stretch them to the full content width.
* NEVER leave a single word alone on its own line (no orphans). If the last line would be one word, keep the last two (or more) words together with a non-breaking space (`&nbsp;`), `<span style="white-space: nowrap">`, or by allowing that line to exceed the 20-word cap. The no-orphan rule wins over the 20-word limit.
* Use `text-wrap: pretty;` on opening prose as a baseline, and still bind the last words when a leftover single word remains.
* Do NOT apply this measure to text inside interactive widgets.

11. CONTEXTUAL BACK NAVIGATION:
* In the bottom sequential navigation (`.ata-sequential-nav` or equivalent), the back/previous link text must NEVER be generic. Do not use labels such as "עמוד קודם", "חזרה", "חזרה לעמוד הקודם", or any other unspecified "back" wording.
* The back link MUST explicitly name the preceding page by its full title or subtitle (the page’s main content heading, typically the `h2` / unit section title — not a vague paraphrase). Prefix with "חזרה ל" when it reads naturally in Hebrew, e.g. "חזרה למטרות החממות והסדנאות".
* Before writing or changing a back link, open the previous page in the sequence, read its exact heading, and use that wording. If the previous page heading is later renamed, update the following page’s back link to match.
* Apply this on every new page and whenever editing existing sequential nav.
